## Metrohm
The exponential rise in the need for better, more efficient power sources has sparked an incredible amount of research into batteries. A primary focus of research has been increasing the energy density of batteries, as it allows for lighter, more portable storage of energy. Lithium-ion batteries, for example, have a much higher energy density than conventional lead-acid batteries and can be used for various purposes, such as in electric vehicles.
## WILEY
Batteries and supercapacitors continue to be one of the most researched topics in the class of energy storage devices. The continuous development of battery and supercapacitor cell components has shown promising development throughout the years—from slabs of pure metal to porous and tailored structures of metal-based active materials. In this direction, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) serve great advantages in improving the properties and structure of the derived metal-based active materials. This research provides a novel electrode material, $N i{-}C o{-}S e/C@C\mathsf{u o}$ derived from Ni–Co-MOF integrated with pre-oxidized $\mathsf{c}_{\mathsf{u}}$ mesh. The superior electrochemical performance of $v_{i-C o-S e/C@C u O}$ over $N i{\mathrm{-}}C o{\cdot}M O F@\mathsf{C u o}$ is evident through its higher specific capacity, lower resistivity, richer redox activity, and more favorable diffusion-dominated storage mechanism. When assembled as a hybrid supercapacitor (HSC), the hybrid device using rGO and Ni–Co– $\mathsf{s e/c}@\mathsf{C u O}$ as electrodes exhibits a high energy density of 42 W $\mathsf{h}\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ at a power density of $2\mathsf{k}\mathsf{w}\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ , and maintains its capacity retention even after 20 000 cycles. The improved capacity performance is also evaluated using first-principle investigations, revealing that the unique and preserved heterostructure of N $\mathsf{i}{-}\mathsf{C o}{-}\mathsf{S e}/\mathsf{C@C u o}$ portrays enhanced metallic properties. Such evaluation of novel electrodes with superior properties may benefit nextgeneration electrodes for supercapacitor devices.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have tailorable properties and have emerged as a captivating crystalline material with $>20000$ structures studied.[1–4] MOFs possess tunable pore sizes ranging from angstroms to almost 10 nanometers and contain a high surface area of $1000{-}10000\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.^{[5}$ –7] With their chemical functionality and porosity, MOFs are promising candidates for catalysis,[8] energy storage/conversion,[9,10] photon exchange membranes,[11] and so on. Generally, MOFs are utilized in two useful functions in supercapacitor (SC) electrode fabrication. First, MOFs are directly used as electrode materials.[12] and second, MOFs are employed as a template for synthesizing different metal sulfides, porous carbons, metal phosphides, metal oxide, and composites.[13] However, the direct application of MOFs as SC electrodes encountered problems mainly related to low chemical stability and electrical conductivity that limit their SC application.[14–17]
Researchers have developed efforts to overcome the issues of poor conductivity and stability of MOFs.[18,19] MOF-derived metallic compound/carbon composites.[20] and transition metal chalcogenides (S, Se, and Te).[21] with unique structures, large surface areas, and tailorable porosity are suitable for overcoming the aforementioned issues. For instance, Ni–Co-based MOFs containing Ni- and Co-rich ions are evaluated with wellcontrolled structure and morphology that can be used as precursors for NiCo-selenide-based electrodes.[22,23] Interesting results have been reported on selenium and/or metal selenide-based electrode materials.[24–29] with most Se-based materials showing unfading capacity during long stability testing and over $80\%$ of their initial Coulombic efficiency.[30] With $1.5\mathrm{~V~}$ of an average sodiation potential versus sodium metal, the theoretical specific capacity of pure Se is 678  mAh $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ .[28] Still, engineering techniques are used to construct metal selenide/C hybrids with a unique structure that can generate appropriate void space and well-contacted interface essential to develop advanced anode materials.[31–33]
The bulk resistance of electrode material is critical in the performance of electrodes.[34–36] Powdery MOFs require the addition of binders and other additives to build a conductive path to the current collector. In a usual preparation, the slurry-coating method is employed, however, resulting in a dead volume that causes bulk resistance.[37,38] This restricts the electrochemical process due to restricted contact between the electrolyte and electrode material.[39] Hence, binder-free electrode preparation shows a promising solution to overcome this problem, as it reduces the resistance between the electrode material and electrolyte by directly growing the active material from the current collector.[40,41] Recently, solution-free synthesis is utilized to synthesize binder-free electrodes, and this process showed great benefits in terms of costs, and production on large-scale with high phase purity. Particularly, Cu has shown lower resistivity and lower cost compared to other current collector candidates, such as Ni- and Ti-based metal current collectors. Moreover, Cu-based grids, foils, and wires have been ideal current collectors as they can be dry-oxidized into CuO nanowires.[36] In our previous work,[9,42,43] $\mathtt{C u O}$ nanowires (NWs) were synthesized by oxidizing Cu mesh without any additional precursor that provided sturdy support to MOF growth. Such technique allows the transformation of the original Cu mesh substrate to CuO nanowires while preserving its flexibility and functions.[44]
In this work, we synthesized free-standing CuO NWs from Cu mesh by simple calcination in the air. Such preparation of the current collector showed a sturdy scaffold for the further integration of Ni–Co-MOF. Then, Ni–Co-MOF $@$ CuO was converted into Ni–Co–Se/C $@$ CuO through simple selenization. The in situ prepared $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C@CuO}$ heterostructure boosted the surface kinetics of the electrode due to the induced internal electric field at the heterointerfaces, which could raise the charge-transfer rate and increase cycling stability. Furthermore, an hybrid supercapacitor (HSC) based on reduced graphene oxide (rGO) and Ni–Co–Se/C@CuO showed excellent electrochemical performance, fulfilling the future requirements for energy storage devices.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The fabrication process of $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ is illustrated in Figure  1. Initially, precursor-free oxidation converts the flat and bare copper mesh into 1D $\mathtt{C u O}$ NWs.[9,45] The solution-free dry oxidation method calcined the Cu mesh into CuO NWs at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The $\mathtt{C u O}$ NWs have ${\approx}20~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ long thin and pointed tips. Such unique structure may increase the adhesion of secondary active materials, and/or may serve as a finer scaffold to further grow secondary nanomaterials.
The morphology of the growth of pristine Cu mesh and Ni– $\mathrm{Co-Se/C@CuO}$ is confirmed by SEM analysis. A bare Cu mesh with a smooth and flat surface can be seen in Figure 2a. After calcining the Cu mesh, a well-organized CuO NWs (Figure 2b) can be observed. Such structure may benefit during polarization, where these NWs may facilitate facile charge transfer to and from the Cu substrate. Meanwhile, the fine nanowire structure can further serve as a template to grow secondary active materials. Figure 2c–e depicts the $\operatorname{Ni-Co-MOF}@\operatorname{CuO}$ consisting of both thin polyhedral structures (Ni–Co-MOF) integrated with $\mathtt{C u O}$ NWs. Further, we selenized the Ni–Co-MOF $@{\mathrm{CuO}}$ into $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C@CuO}$ wherein the thick polyhedral structure was still oriented obtained (Figure  2f–h). The coated layer thickness is suggested to be ${\approx}15{-}20~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ from a cross-sectional view (Figure  2f,g). At higher magnification, $\mathtt{C u O}$ NWs can still be observed as shown with its tips protruding out from the combined structure (Figure  2h). The homogeneous distribution of elements of the hybrid structure $(\mathrm{Ni-Co-Se/C@CuO})$ is confirmed through energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). It can be depicted that the elements C, Cu, O, Ni, Co, and Se are uniformly distributed, and the color intensity is proportional to the element cluster in the compound. The EDX spectrum for Ni–Co-MOF@CuO is given in Figure S1 (Supporting Information), confirming the presence of C, Cu, O, Ni, and Co elements. As described in previous studies.[46] the vapor-solid growth mechanism from Cu mesh indicates the formation of $\mathtt{C u O}$ . As shown in the XRD spectra (Figure S2a, Supporting Information), different peaks were observed in the $\mathrm{Ni-Co-Se/C@}$ CuO that correspond to the combined peaks from Cu (JCPDS no. 01-085-1326), CuO (JCPDS no. 01-080-1268), and Ni–Co–Se (29-1417). The in situ growth of $\mathrm{Ni{-}C o{-}S e/C@C u O}$ caused a slight shift toward the lower angle that may be due to the integration of Ni–Co–Se in the CuO scaffold. Figure S2b (Supporting Information) depicts the elements C, Cu, O, Ni, Co, and Se present in the hybrid $\mathrm{Ni-Co-Se/C@CuO}$ , which also revealed the homogenous distribution of elements as described in Figure 2i. The high-resolution TEM images and SAED reveal
clear fringes with interplanar spacing of $0.26\ \mathrm{\nm}/0.23\ \mathrm{\nm}$ , polycrystalline nature of the Ni–Co–Se/C@CuO (Figure S2c, orresponding to the (210/111) planes of Ni–Co–Se/CuO and Supporting Information).
The chemical bonding of the synthesized material was examined by using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Cu $2\mathrm{p}$ peaks consist of two spin-orbit doublets positioned for $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Cu $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ at the binding energies of 931.7 and 951.7  eV, respectively (Figure  3a). These doublets show the presence of two different valence states of $\mathrm{Cu}$ as ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . $[47]$ The $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ valance state of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ was confirmed by the presence of satellite peaks at higher binding energies.[9] Two primary peaks for $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}$ located at 861.4 and $873.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ , respectively (Figure  3b). The spin-orbit of the Co 2p spectrum split into $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ at the binding energies of 796.5 and $780.9{\mathrm{~eV}},$ respectively (Figure  3c).[48] Meanwhile, the Gaussian fitting of O 1s indicates the existence of three characteristic peaks at the binding energies of ${\approx}530.5$ (OI), ${\approx}530.8\$ (OII), and ${\approx}532$ (OIII) eV, which correspond to the metal-oxygen bond, surface oxygen, and adsorbed water on the surface, respectively (Figure  3d). The fitted peak for C 1s (Figure  3e) at $284.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to sp.[3] carbon, and the other peaks can be attributed to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-O}}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ at 285.5 and $286.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. Moreover, the presence of Se was also confirmed, as shown in Figure  3f, indicating the successful selenization of the MOF precursors.
The electrochemical performance of Ni–Co-MOF@CuO and $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ was evaluated by using cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD), and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) using the three-electrode system with $3\mathrm{~m~}$ KOH as electrolyte. The comparative CV peaks $(10\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1})}$ of $\operatorname{Ni-Co-MOF}@\operatorname{CuO}$ and $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ are shown in Figure 4a with a potential operational window of $0.6\mathrm{V}$ versus $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ . A quasi-reversible peak was observed ${\approx}0.49$ versus $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ , which could be referred to as the oxidation of Co and Ni species. During the negative scan, a reduction peak was observed ${\approx}0.38\mathrm{~V~}$ versus $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ . This behavior was observed for both $\operatorname{Ni-Co-MOF}@\operatorname{CuO}$ and $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ material. The broader and wider area of the CV curve for Ni–Co– $\mathsf{S e/C@C u O}$ relates to its better performance compared to Ni– Co-MOF $@{\mathrm{CuO}}$ . The GCD curves of both Ni–Co-MOF@CuO and $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}{\widehat{\underline{{a}}}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}\mathbf{O}$ are measured at the current density of 2 A $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ over a potential window of $0.6~\mathrm{V}.$ Figure 4b depicts the comparison curves of the GCD test and the $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ recorded a higher discharge time than Ni–Co-MOF $@$ CuO. The calculated specific capacitance of Ni–Co–Se/C@CuO was $640\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ that is $240\%$ higher than Ni–Co-MOF@CuO. The detailed CV and GCD at different scan rate curves of Ni– ${\mathrm{Co-MOF}}@{\mathrm{CuO}}$ (Figure S3, Supporting Information) and Ni– $\mathrm{Co-Se/C@CuO}$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information) are given in Supporting Informations. Figure 4c depicts the detailed specific capacities of $\operatorname{Ni-Co-MOF}@\operatorname{CuO}$ and $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ at various current densities, exhibiting the superior performance of $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ . The detailed calculations for capacitance are given in the supplemental information.[49]
Power law was used to evaluate the charge storage mechanism in electrodes.[50] The fitted areas of CV curves (Figure  4d,e) elaborate the diffusion and capacitive contribution of Ni–Co-MOF $@$ CuO and $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ respectively. In the comparison of both materials, Ni–Co– MOF $@{\mathsf{C u O}}$ and $\mathrm{Ni{-}C o{-}S e/C@C u O}$ , the capacitive contribution was reduced from 69 to $31\%$ and the diffusion control was boosted from 31 to $69\%$ . This clearly suggests that the response for $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ is dominated by the diffusion-redox reactions that were responsible for such an increase in capacitance. The Nyquist plot is represented by the slope depicted from the lower frequency region, and a depressed semi-circle at the higher frequency region. From the Nyquist plot (Figure  4f ), the lack of semi-circle at the higher frequency region strongly suggests that the charge transfer resistance is negligible. Meanwhile, the ESR for Ni– $\mathrm{Co-Se/C@CuO}$ (1.15  ohm) is lower than Ni–Co-MOF@CuO (1.18  ohm). Furthermore, the Warburg element represents the diffusion impedance of the electrolyte ions to the pores of the electrode surface. Herein, the plot for $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ showed a closer slope to the $\gamma$ axis suggesting better diffusion of ions compared to Ni–Co-MOF $@$ CuO. Cycling stability is one of the key elements to determine the practical application of electrode material. The cycling stability of the electrodes was measured with GCD for 20 000 cycles at the rate of $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . A Coulombic efficiency of $99.3\%$ and capacity of $91.7\%$ were retained, as shown in Figure 4g. The comparison of $\mathrm{Ni{-}C o{-}S e/C\mathcal{Q}C u O^{\prime}}$ s stability with other published work is shown in Figure 4h and Table S1 (Supporting Information).
The enhanced performance of $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ could be attributed to the following factors (Figure 4i). First, Cu mesh aided the formation of a unique nanowire-filled scaffold that allowed a better integration of the Ni–Co-MOF@CuO. The Ni–Co-MOF benefitted from a porous property that permitted its structure preservation even when converted to $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ . The successful anchoring and conversion granted a beneficial heterostructure that connected Ni–Co–Se/C and $\mathtt{C u O}$ into an integrated architecture. Second, the incorporation of selenide empowered supplemental energy storage activities when compared to its MOF form. This resulted in Ni–Co–Se/C@CuO to function with diffusion-dominant behavior. Lastly, the conversion to selenide all while maintaining the redox-rich active material with tailorable voids allowed a highly stable structure beneficial for long-term cycling. Hence, Ni–Co–Se/C@CuO showed superior performance than its MOF form requiring further evaluation as electrodes for HSC device.
The schematic illustration of the as-prepared hybrid SC is shown in Figure  5a, which represents rGO and $\mathrm{Ni-Co-Se/C@CuO}$ as electrodes. Briefly, rGO worked toward the negative potential $(-1.0{-}0\mathrm{~V})$ while the $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ worked on the positive potential (0–0.6 V; Figure 5b). Upon balancing the charges and when assembled as a device, the Ni– $\mathrm{Co-Se/C@CuO}$ and rGO delivered a stable potential window of $1.6\mathrm{V}$ as shown from the scan rate of $10\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ (Figure 5c). After determining the maximum potential window (1.6 V), the GCD was examined at different current densities starting from 2 to 10 A $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure 5d). Ragone plot depicts the energy and power density of the cell and its comparison with published articles (Figure 5e; Table S2, Supporting Information). From the capacitance calculated from the GCD profile (Figure 5d), a maximum energy density of 42 W h $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ at a power density of $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{~kW~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{kg}}^{-1}$ was obtained. The cycling stability of the cell was evaluated for 20 000 GCD cycles resulting in a Coulombic efficiency retained at $100\%$ . On the other note, the capacitance was initially increased in the first 2500 cycles, which may refer to the initial activation of the cell.[51] At the end of the $20000\mathrm{GCD}$ cycle, the capacity was retained at $101\%$ . Such performance of $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}@$ $\mathtt{C u O}$ as an effective electrode showed its feasibility with promising stability for practical application.
To further understand the properties of the electrode, the structural and superior electrochemical performance of $\mathtt{C u O}$ embedded with $\mathrm{Ni-Co-Se/C}$ were verified computationally by using first principles DFT calculations. Figure  6a shows the optimized structures of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{S e}_{4}$ (Figure  6a(i)), CuO (Figure 6a(ii)), and $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}@\mathrm{CuO}$ (Figure 6a(iii)). Figure 6a(i) indicates that the atomic structure of $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ is made up of the $\mathrm{NiSe}_{4}$ tetrahedral and $\mathrm{CoSe}_{4}$ polyhedral motifs. Each poly­ hedron was connected to the nearby octahedron by corner sharing. Meanwhile, the octahedra were connected to themselves by edge-sharing. The $_\mathrm{Ni-Se}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{Se}}$ bond distances were calculated to be 2.29 and $2.37\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , respectively. Figure 6a(ii) depicts the model unit cell of the bulk $\mathtt{C u O}$ of which the structure was constituted by the planar $\mathrm{CuO}_{4}$ motif with the $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ bond distance of $1.96\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . These unit cells of the constituent materials were subsequently used to construct the heterostructure of $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}@\mathrm{CuO}$ as shown in Figure 6a(iii). Here, we modeled CuO by exposing its (001) surface to $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ because it is one of the facets found in CuO NWs.[52] As a finite structure on the host $\mathtt{C u O}$ , the atomic structure of $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ is distorted as indicated by the relative change in the bond distance of ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{Se}}$ in ${\mathrm{CoSe}}_{6}$ and the reduction in the coordination number of Ni from 4 to 2 as compared with those of initial $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ . The Co and Se atoms from $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ were connected with O atoms of $\mathtt{C u O}$ , and the shortest $\mathrm{Co-O}$ and $\mathsf{S e{\mathrm{-}}O}$ bond distances are 3.34 and $3.28\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively, indicating that both the materials weakly interacted with each other. Hence, they preserved their crystal structures, as supported by the XRD spectra presented earlier (Figure S2a, Supporting Information).
The electronic partial density of states (PDOS) of i) ${\mathrm{NiCo}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{4},$ ii) $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ in $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}@\mathrm{CuO}$ , and iii) $\mathtt{C u O}$ in $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}@\mathrm{CuO}$ are shown in Figure  6b. The original $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ were found to be not spin-polarized (i.e., symmetrical PDOS of the spin-up and spin-down channels). As for $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ in $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}@\mathrm{CuO}$ (Figure 6b(ii)), this material became spin-polarized after mixing with host $\mathtt{C u O}$ . This can be attributed to the presence of dangling bonds and crystal deformation in the finite structure of $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ . Moreover, the host $\mathtt{C u O}$ now adopts a magnetic metallic conductor behavior as indicated by the highly delocalized electronic states of $\mathrm{Cu}$ and O atoms near the Fermi energy. In particular, this conductive nature of $\mathtt{C u O}$ in $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{S e}_{4}$ differed from the non-magnetic semiconducting bulk,[53] and it may profit from a better charge transfer, hence, boosting the $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{4}@\mathrm{CuO}$ electrode’s high-rate performance.
## 3. Conclusion
In this study, the $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ was synthesized by three-step synthesis. In the first step, free-standing CuO nanowires from Cu mesh were prepared through the dry oxidation method. In the second step, the Ni–Co-MOF@CuO was integrated with $\mathtt{C u O}$ through a solvothermal approach. Lastly, the MOF-based electrode was selenized to Ni–Co– $\mathsf{S e/C@C u O}$ . The heterostructure between the 3D Ni–Co–Se/C and 1D CuO created a conductive structure that facilitated better charge transfer that eventually lead to a high-rate performance. Furthermore, the binder-free conductive structure allowed the full utilization of free volumes and spaces. The hybrid device exhibited excellent stability of $\approx100\%$ after 20 000 cycles and a high energy density of 42  W  h $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ at a power density of $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{~kW~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{kg}}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, atomic-scale simulations based on density functional theory verified the structural properties of the electrode, which further explained the achieved enhancement in the electrochemical properties of $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ . These excellent electrochemical results made $\operatorname{Ni-Co-Se/C}@\operatorname{CuO}$ attractive as next-generation electrodes in energy storage devices.
